== recovery net ==
hardware watchdog: ARMED. The SoC resets itself if the payload stops making
                   progress, whatever the CPU is doing - and platform_reboot()
                   forces a bite so the reboot does not depend on PS_HOLD.
software dead-man: armed (60s), as a second net.
MEASURED, this arm: the last four hangs (517's first run, 521, 522, 526) did NOT come back
         and each needed a power press - 526's port has been silent for hours against a bite
         due 28 s after the arming. Why they did not come back is NOT measured: the entry
         image carries no address in the watchdog's page, which is checked below, but that is
         not the same as unreachable - the watchdog shares its 1 MB section with the GIC, and
         this image maps that section. Expect a power press, and expect the log to go with it.

== the net's reach in the entry image ==
  /mnt/data/mi4-ios6/out/stage90/xnu_arm_entry.bin:
    1582869 aligned words, 51201 carrying the movt encoding (a ceiling)
    [0xf9010000, 0xf901ffff]   pool word 0   movt rD, #0xf901 0   mov/mvn immediate 0
    witness, one nibble below - the GIC, which this image does use:
    [0xf9000000, 0xf900ffff]   pool word 0   movt rD, #0xf900 20   mov/mvn immediate 4
      the movt counts are ceilings for the same reason: the mask frees the condition field,
      so a data word sharing the low half is counted too.
    exempt code (entry_wdt_pet), which a residence arm must carry the page in:
      4 page reach(es) - pool word 0   movt rD, #0xf901 4   mov/mvn immediate 0
  positive control - the payload, which arms the net, so it must carry the page:
    /mnt/data/mi4-ios6/out/stage90/stage90.bin
    pool word 0   movt rD, #0xf901 10   mov/mvn immediate 0
  and nothing in this image pets the net, so the run is CAPPED: a run of this arm that goes well
  also ends within the net's own interval, not when the payload stops. The two constants are read
  straight from `src/entry/stage90.h` rather than quoted here:
    STAGE90_HW_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_S    29 s   (the bark)
    STAGE90_HW_WATCHDOG_BITE_GAP_S   3 s   (bark -> bite)
    so the bite is at most 32 s after the payload arms it, and the payload
    spends about 1 s of that before the jump (measured: 533's payload timebase sample to the
    idle's first read is 1.1805 s on one 19.2 MHz counter).
  READ IT AS 'UNUSED', NOT 'UNREACHABLE'. This image maps the watchdog's registers on every boot -
  the GIC probe's own 1 MB section descriptor (0xf901040e, base 0xf9000000, attr index 3 =
  strongly-ordered) covers 0xf9017000, and both archived captures carry it. What the scan above
  measures is that nothing STORES to them, and a store is what a pet is. Changing this ceiling is
  therefore not new machinery: it is one store to 0xf9017004 (WDT0_RST) from a path XNU already
  runs, in a wrapper this image already has. That is a decision about the arm and not part of it.
  MEASURED, what that ceiling looks like: 513's two captures - the same regime, an image with no
  repair - end their payload records at the fifth poll with panic 0, Attempting system restart 0,
  MACH Reboot 0, pc_sample_watchdog_fired 0 and platform_reboot entered 0, so NO software path
  ended either run and the records simply stop after the work, with no fault text. Read a capture
  of this arm that way: 'the log ends without a fault' is the ceiling, not a missing reading.
== the boot argument that selects the idle arm ==
549: SKIPPED on D13 - the idle-cache boot argument does not exist in this tree.
  D13's arm_init parses only maxmem/-no-cache/serial and ships no caches.c; its idle is gated
  by do_power_save (pmCPU.c:41), a compile default, not a boot argument. The boot image is
  confirmed NOT to carry up_style_idle_exit=1, so no idle arm is being claimed.

== mode policy ==
HARD_SKIP: stops before the candidate L1, the watchdog loop and the jump.

== ladder ==
FULL (4): boot-args, early pmap, PE_init_platform, post-PE bootstrap, arm_vm_init,
          the high-VA windows and the loader. The only level that reaches the handoff.

== fault injection ==
off: the handoff targets the Stage-owned high-VA function as usual.

== entering XNU ==
ENTERING XNU: the payload copies a linked image containing XNU's real osfmk/arm/start.s
          to PA 0x80000000 (experiment 241's base; 0x00200000 before it), hands it a
          boot_args (physBase == virtBase), and jumps.
          Nothing after that jump is the payload's: the page tables, vectors, caches
          and MMU state are XNU's, and the payload never runs again.

          Endings, and the log says which:
            '...real XNU entry: _start ran to completion and branched to arm_init'
                 XNU's own entry sequence ran on this device, end to end.
            '...real XNU entry: exception: <which>'
                 it faulted, and the vector names itself.
            neither, ending at 'jumping to XNU's _start'
                 it hung; the hardware watchdog brings the phone back in ~28s.
            no log at all, and the device does not come back
                 the net below did not recover it and the ram_console died with the
                 cold boot; the phone needs a power-button press and the run is lost.
                 This is not hypothetical and it is not rare enough to ignore: it is
                 experiment 517's first run (2026-09-21). One observation, and the
                 cause is not known - the run had two changes in it, so the next image
                 carries one (see STAGE90_XNU_EXIT_POC_FLUSH in build_entry.sh).
                 A candidate mechanism exists and is now measured rather than
                 reasoned (experiments 517/518): the interrupt handler's stack starts
                 at cpu_data->istackptr, which is also where the idle loop runs, so
                 the handler's 5th and 6th pushed words land on the return address
                 the idle exit is about to pop - and 518's run shows it popping a
                 timebase value into the PC. 518's arm (move istackptr to the middle
                 of the interrupt stack) RAN on 2026-09-22 and could not test it:
                 istackptr is read by the vector AND by the context switch that puts
                 the idle thread on that stack, so the arm moved both and the gap is
                 invariant (SS_SP == istackptr - 16 in 516 and in 518 alike). The
                 same panic returned, with the device recovering on its own. The arm
                 cannot be another value of that field, so 519 changes where the idle
                 thread's stack comes from instead: Idle_context is the one place that
                 choice is made, and the arm rewrites its ldr sp from
                 cpu_data->istackptr into a load of this image's own 16 KB array - so
                 the handler keeps the whole interrupt stack and the idle body runs
                 outside [intstack_top - 16384, intstack_top), which is exactly what
                 ml_at_interrupt_context() tests. (518 section 5 proposed
                 __wrap_Idle_load_context; that hook restores sp from the thread's pcb,
                 so it cannot choose the idle body's stack - experiment 519 section 1.)
                 **519 RAN on 2026-09-22 and obtained both of its predictions.**
                 The arm worked: xnu_live_idlestack_sp = 0x8054fea8 is inside
                 [stage90_idle_stack, +16384) with xnu_live_idlestack_inwin = 0 and
                 _calls = 1, so the idle body is out of the window the kernel's
                 predicate tests - and the live chain agrees with the panic's own sp
                 from two sides (array top 0x8054fee0, cpu_idle's 'sub sp, sp, #8',
                 the exit wrapper's 8-byte frame => the exit's entry sp is
                 0x8054fed0). And the run ends in the *diagnosed* panic, not
                 Apple's flat one: 'sleh_abort: prefetch abort in kernel mode:
                 fault_addr=0x7152a6c' with frame_ok = 1, fsr_frame = 5,
                 sp 0x8054fed0, lr 0x800462dc, pc = far = 0x07152a6c - i.e.
                 ml_at_interrupt_context() answered false. The panel is the idle
                 path's own registers one instruction before the pop (r0/r1 are
                 the exit's own str, r5 and r4 are cpu_idle's, r4 = the value of
                 cpu_data->rtcPop), so the two words the pop read are two counter
                 readings one tick apart, the later one the deadline the idle loop
                 held. 519 section 10 removes 518's mechanism by arithmetic:
                 EXC_CTX_SIZE is 360 (80 + 264 + 16, genassym.c:188) and the VFP
                 area never ends above the interrupted sp, so no exception frame
                 writes above the sp it interrupted - the writer has to be
                 WATCHED, not reasoned about. **520 RAN on 2026-09-22 and watched it.**
                 That arm was 519's switch set unchanged with seven live-channel keys
                 added per site and no state change anywhere. The death reproduced at
                 the same instruction - xnu_live_sleh_storm = 9, sp = 0x8054fed0,
                 lr = 0x800462dc, numerically identical to 519's - and the writer is
                 now a number rather than a candidate: xnu_live_slot_rtcpre_pop =
                 0x04b79075 is cpu_data->rtcPop read immediately before the fatal
                 call, and the fault's pc = 0x04b79074 is rtcPop - 1, the Thumb
                 relationship 519 read off its own r4/far pair. lr = 0x800462dc is
                 the return address of platform_cache_idle_exit's bl FlushPoU_Dcache
                 (0x800462d8) and its bcc at 0x80046300 is taken, so the faulting
                 instruction is that function's own pop {fp, pc} at 0x8004633c - a
                 pop, which is why lr still names the flush. The deadline reaches
                 that word only via strd r4, [sp, #-12]! in
                 __wrap_platform_cache_idle_enter (0x8047c8d4) and
                 __wrap_cpu_idle_wfi (0x8047c888): pre-indexed 12 from the same X
                 the exit wrapper's 8-byte frame lands on, so r4 goes to E-4, the
                 slot's UPPER word. The exit's own push writes it first and still
                 loses, because the window is open: caches.c:406 clears SCTLR.C,
                 so the push reaches DRAM and touches neither the valid L1 line nor
                 the L2 - 516's enter-side CleanPoC_Dcache is DCCSW (c7,c10,2),
                 which CLEANS and leaves the line VALID - then caches.c:490 sets
                 SCTLR.C again and the pop's load MISSES nothing, it hits. Apple's
                 FlushPoU_Dcache is DCCISW, the right operation, but L1 only.
                 The repair is FlushPoC_Dcache (DCCISW over L1 AND L2), which is
                 exactly the call 517 added at this site behind
                 STAGE90_XNU_EXIT_POC_FLUSH and which has never been run on its own.
                 **So the arm to run is the exit-side PoC flush, switch on, with 520's
                 instrument repaired per its section 11** - the two slot loads moved
                 into the wrapper's inline asm (they were read through
                 entry_slot_note's own frame: pre_m4 = 0x8047c974 is that note's own
                 saved lr), the abort site publishing every abort instead of a
                 geometric subsequence (the fatal seq 9 was neither <= 4 nor a power
                 of two and its SS_SP 0x8054fed0 was INSIDE the guard - the one
                 reading the arm existed for was sampled away), and the window
                 widened to the kernel map's own bounds with the rtc note's inner
                 refusals counted. The prediction is the panic's ABSENCE, not
                 a further reading: the push then writes the only copy there is and
                 the pop reads DRAM. Reading 4 answered its own question unused:
                 ml_get_timebase was called >= 1024 times, 517's instrument
                 published no xnu_live_tb_* record at all (its early return on
                 SCTLR.C set), and its own pair spans exactly 24 ticks - so it is
                 not the source of a 1-tick pair.
                 **That arm ran (521) and the device did not come back.** One
                 non-persistent fastboot boot, 'Booting OKAY', and then no adb, no
                 fastboot, no getvar - a power press, and the log lost with the cold
                 boot. The second such non-return in the project and the second
                 flag-on image, which is what leaves the flush as the only remaining
                 difference from the flag-off runs that all come back: a full L1 AND
                 L2 clean-and-invalidate inside the idle window, with SCTLR.C clear,
                 is the one operation this hardware has never survived - 517's first
                 run carried the same call and also needed a power press.
                 **So the arm to run is 522: turn the D-cache back ON at the window's
                 near end and take the flush out of the picture.**
                 entry_idle_cache_enable does the one thing Apple's own clear does in
                 reverse - read SCTLR, orr #4, write it back, dsb, isb - and
                 __wrap_platform_cache_idle_enter calls it the moment the real
                 platform_cache_idle_enter returns (0x8047c924), before the WFI. That
                 is a coherence property rather than a maintenance operation: the
                 WFI, the exit's push {fp, lr} and the pop {fp, pc} that 519 and 520
                 died in all run with the cache on, so a store updates the line a
                 later load reads instead of landing in DRAM behind a line nothing
                 had invalidated. The exit-side flush is OFF in this image, so the
                 one change from 521 is the cache enable - and a run that does not
                 come back after this is a datum rather than a failure to fix: the
                 next arm is a null instrument (the same wrapper with the readings
                 replaced by a counter) to separate the cost of the measurements
                 from the cost of the state change.
                 Four things to read, and the first is the verdict: (1) no
                 'sleh_abort' panic and no xnu_live_sleh_storm growth past 520's
                 9 - the idle exit retires its own epilogue; (2)
                 xnu_live_slot_cwe_win shows C clear and xnu_live_slot_cwe_set shows
                 it set, with _calls >= 1 - the enable ran inside the window and
                 really took; (3) xnu_live_slot_post_calls >= 1, which says the exit
                 RETURNED through the wrapper (520's was 0, its pass dying inside the
                 call); (4) any later ending than 520's is progress - a different pc
                 or the boot moving on - and the same death at the same pc says the
                 mechanism is not the cache state but something the window itself
                 does.
                 **The 522 arm ran on 2026-09-22 and nothing came back to adb** (section 3.3
                 of experiment 522 has the USB timeline: dev 88 = 18d1:d00d at 14:14:45, gone at
                 14:14:46, nothing after) - and 551 is the repair of how that was concluded,
                 because the same d00d-then-gone shape appears in the host log followed by a
                 genuine return ([2163244] through [2173264], serial 4a2fe00b), which is why
                 run_and_capture.sh now enters 2 only when the log itself shows no new enumeration
                 of the serial - and 3, a capture failure and not a hang, when it does. Either way
                 the prediction above is spent and the four readings it named have no values: if
                 the device never came back there was no log, and if it came back unread the run
                 was not the arm's run. What the run did produce is a
                 ledger, and it is the reason the arm to run now is different in kind rather than
                 in value: 521 and 522 both carry 521's repair of the exit wrapper's capture (the
                 four words of the idle exit's {fp, lr} slot read by the caller at each of two
                 sites, 8 loads and 8 stores a pass) and BOTH failed to come back, while 520 -
                 which carries neither that capture nor any state change - came back; 521 and 522
                 differ only in which state change they carry, the exit-side FlushPoC_Dcache and
                 the enter-side SCTLR.C re-enable, so the capture is present in every image that
                 did not return and absent from every one that did.
                 **So the arm to run is 526: the null instrument.** Its image is 522's with the
                 capture replaced by a counter - STAGE90_XNU_SLOT_NULL=1, which takes the four
                 loads and the four pend_* stores out of each capture site and leaves
                 entry_slot_null_note publishing the pass count alone; the exit-side flush stays
                 at 0 and everything else in the wrapper (the 8-byte frame, the single
                 mov r?, sp, 516's CleanPoC_Dcache, 519's rtcPop reading, the one call to the
                 real exit, and 522's re-enable in the enter wrapper) is unchanged. That was
                 checked rather than intended: the same tree built with STAGE90_XNU_SLOT_NULL=0
                 reproduces 522's image 13d771938336fe65... byte for byte, so the two images
                 differ by the capture and by nothing else.
                 What to read, in the order that matters: (1) **does the device come back** - that
                 is the whole experiment, and a return says the readings cost something (and the
                 next arm bisects them: the two loads before the call, or the two after it) while a
                 third non-return says the cost is the state change 521 and 522 do not share; (2) if
                 it returns, xnu_live_slot_pre_calls and xnu_live_slot_post_calls are present and
                 rising with the four words ABSENT - the site ran and published a count and nothing
                 else, which is what a null run should look like, and their absence is the other
                 reading (the site never ran); (3) the enable: whether the SCTLR.C write is in
                 this image at all - and therefore whether the _cwe_* pair is a test or a
                 formality - is what the derived line above the XNU-entry block reads out of
                 the record, and that line governs this one; where the write IS in the image,
                 522's block in run_and_capture.sh --summarise reads this log for it too -
                 xnu_live_slot_cwe_win shows C clear, _set shows it set with _calls >= 1; (4) the
                 ending, and here the arm decides what the ending means rather than the ending
                 deciding the arm: 547 section 4 pre-registers this death for the enable-off arm -
                 the pass reaches the exit, the push runs with C clear, the pop loads the stale
                 words and the prefetch abort panics - so on that arm a death whose lr is the
                 address the exit's own bl FlushPoU_Dcache returns to is the *prediction* and not
                 a negative result, and what falsifies 546's mechanism is a **return with no pop
                 death at all**, which is the one reading that forbids 535 as designed. Which
                 cell this log is in, and whether the death is the predicted shape, is what
                 run_and_capture.sh --summarise decides - it derives that address from this
                 image's own ELF at run time and separates the two by the log's published
                 xnu_live_sleh_lr, so nothing above restates the rule and a death read here by
                 eye is read that way and not by this sentence.
                 What this arm costs, read off 520's own log rather than assumed: that log's
                 per-pass publishers have exactly one record each
                 (xnu_live_slot_pre_calls, xnu_live_slot_rtcpre_calls, xnu_live_sip_seq and the
                 xnu_live_pce_seq/_after_seq pair), while the door counter's last published record
                 is 32768 - so the cache window itself was entered once, and that one entry is the
                 death. A run of this arm gets one pass at the exit, so a non-return costs a power
                 press and nothing else, which is why this phase is expensive in boots and not in
                 passes. And the item that localises the death is the *bracket*, not the ending:
                 pre and rtcpre published with xnu_live_slot_post_calls absent is what puts the
                 death inside platform_cache_idle_exit, with no second pass to confuse it. (Line
                 numbers quoted from that log are readable within their own block only: the ram
                 console's records and its console text are two writers into two blocks, so a
                 line's number says nothing about its order against a line in the other one.)
                 The image this preflight is being run against is
                 dd91a25fb9edbb76... , computed from the file itself two screens up.

          Safety: the hardware watchdog is the only net across the jump, deliberately
          - the software dead-man needs the payload's GIC and vector state, which are
          gone the moment _start switches tables. The watchdog needs neither.
          Its record, read from the ledgers rather than from this text: it recovered
          every run from 506 to 515, including runs parked in the kernel's own idle
          WFI and runs in an abort storm, and 516's two runs came back on XNU's own
          'MACH Reboot'. It then did not recover 517's first run, and the two runs
          whose non-return is what chose the current arm - 521 and 522 - are further
          misses; net-pessimistic, and the route has drifted with the ledgers, so
          what stands is the verdict and not the count. So: a net that has held many
          times and is not proved to hold always - and not the way a non-return is
          judged either, since 551 reads that out of the host log (exit 2 against
          exit 3), which is a reading the net cannot give. A hang here may need a
          power press, and the device is never at risk of being bricked - nothing in
          this project is ever written to storage.

