== what run_and_capture.sh's exit status can mean ==
read out of that section at gate time: 5 exit site(s) (3 written 'exit N', 2
  written as a 'die' call whose code is die()'s own), distinct code(s) 1 2 3, at
    run_and_capture.sh:3976   exit 3
    run_and_capture.sh:3999   die -> exit 1
    run_and_capture.sh:4037   die -> exit 1
    run_and_capture.sh:4046   exit 2
    run_and_capture.sh:4089   exit 2
which code means which state is that file's own wording at those lines - read them there
the 2 die site(s) above exit 1 - read out of die()'s definition, because a die
call does not carry its code on the line. In this section that code has two producers and neither
owes a power press: the host could not read its own USB log (a reading of the host failing, not of
the device), and the boot call reporting no success (so a non-return after it is not a verdict
about the payload). Both refuse to call the run a non-return - the press is owed only by the code
whose own message says the device did not come back. The lines above name each producer's state in
that file's own words, and the code set is checked against this gate below; the *count* of
producers within one code is not checked, so a third one would change this sentence silently.
for scope: this file has 19 exit site(s) in total over both spellings, code(s) 0 1 2 3 -
  so the count and the codes above are section 4's, and a code can have a second producer outside it.
So a non-return is not one thing in that section: it distinguishes states, and its codes are
not interchangeable - one of them says the device *did* come back, into a state adb cannot
reach, which is a capture failure and not a hang. Before recording any of them, confirm
against the host's own log, by serial and not by port (this host's port has a second occupant
that appears on its own after hours of silence, so a port-only test reads *that* as a return):
  sudo dmesg | grep 4a2fe00b                 # bare dmesg prints nothing on this host
and remember what that log cannot say: a payload that never returned and a phone whose Android
failed to come up are the same reading in adb alone, which is why exit 2 was too wide a claim.
Only the code whose own message says the device did not come back owes a power press - not the
one that refuses to call this a non-return - and the log at /tmp/cancro-last_kmsg.txt survives only until that
press, so read it first.
== the log those instructions name, as it stands at gate time ==
  /tmp/cancro-last_kmsg.txt
  this is a reading of THIS moment, not a claim about the run: the gate cannot see whether a run
  follows it, and run_and_capture.sh's step 2b parks this file before the boot (that file's own
  564). So after the run the test is not this number: it is whether /tmp/cancro-last_kmsg.txt is there at all, and
  whether what is there is non-empty - the hand retry exit 3's message asks for is a shell
  redirect, which creates the file before adb can fail. One state is the exception and the gate
  cannot see it from here, because whether the park *succeeds* turns on /tmp's sticky bit and the
  file's identity (511/512): if step 2b could not move this file aside, the earlier log stays at
  this name, a capture that fails leaves it looking untouched, and *then* the fingerprint below is
  the test - the runner says so in its own words when it takes that branch.
  And the earlier log is not lost when this name is gone after the run: step 2b parks the file
  at /tmp/cancro-last_kmsg.txt.prev (then .prev.2, ...) whenever there is one to park, and that path is where an
  earlier bracket lives. Read it for the earlier death and never as this run's, whatever the
  bracket in it says - and read 'gone with nothing parked' as the capture it is, not as the park
  it is not: with nothing at this name at gate time (the absent case below) step 2b has nothing to
  move, so an absence after the run is this run's capture having failed, not an earlier log tucked
  away.
  absent - and that is the useful part: after the run, this file *existing* is itself the first
  check, because nothing here writes it before a capture succeeds. Step 2b says the same thing
  about this exact state ('no log at ... yet'), and step 5 reads into /tmp/cancro-last_kmsg.txt.new and renames it
  into place only on a non-empty read - so a name still absent afterwards captured nothing,
  whatever the exit code said.
