== which arm the entry image in out/ is, in words ==
  idle sleep (IDLE_NO_SLEEP=0): the arm that came back - 514's one repair IS in this image, so
      `SIGPdisabled` is cleared once from `__wrap_poll` on pid 1's thread before the park's real
      call and `cpu_idle` can leave by its SECOND door: **the window whose `pop {fp, pc}` is this
      phase's frontier IS ENTERED, so everything below - the slot, the bracket, the window's two
      ends and the seam - is narration about THIS arm, and its keys are expected PRESENT.** 533's
      capture carries `xnu_live_slot_cwe_win` and `xnu_live_pce_after_sctlr`, which is the window
      having been entered and read; 520's carries neither because its image predates the instrument,
      which is why the pair and not one of them settles what this arm does.
      **On this arm an absent `xnu_live_repair_seq` is a bug rather than the switch** - the
      converse of what the 1 arm's block says, and the pair is the reason this gate prints the arm's
      value from the record before any run rather than leaving it to be inferred from the log after.
      Its death is the one both parked captures show and the reason this phase has a frontier at
      all: the exit's `push {fp, lr}` / `pop {fp, pc}` inside `platform_cache_idle_exit`, with the
      park's poll never returning. The park group's
      `cpu_signal_handler_internal(FALSE) called 1 time(s)` is therefore **absent for that same
      reason** - it prints only after the park's real poll returns, so on this arm the group's
      absence says the park did not return and not that the repair was skipped.
  capture sites (SLOT_NULL=1): the NULL instrument - entry_slot_null_note publishes the pass
      count alone, so the four slot words are neither loaded nor stored by this image and the
      xnu_live_slot_{pre,post}_{sp,m16,m12,m8,m4} keys are expected ABSENT, not missing.
      So a run of this arm prints two UNREAD lines for the slot's own capture, naming
      xnu_live_slot_pre_calls - that is this switch and not a disagreement, and a successful
      boot of this arm carries them. Read them as 'the comparison could not be made here'.
  bracket (pre note -> rtcpre note -> the real exit -> post note; 538 read the three call sites out of
      these very bytes): xnu_live_slot_pre_calls, xnu_live_slot_rtcpre_calls and
      xnu_live_slot_post_calls are published by the exit wrapper in the same pass, on one schedule
      and one gate - so pre without rtcpre localizes the death between the two notes, and pre and
      rtcpre without post puts it inside platform_cache_idle_exit, which is the localization this
      arm exists to buy. **xnu_live_slot_rtcab_* is NOT part of this bracket.** entry_stubs.c:1792
      publishes it from entry_note_sleh, the *abort* path, so it counts abort records and not
      entries: in 520's own log it has five (1,2,3,4,8), i.e. at least eight aborts, and a bracket
      read off it would put the window at eight entries when the exit wrapper's rtcpre has exactly
      one record. The entry-side pair is entry_trace.c:1973's rtcpre beside :1971's pre, and 556's
      narration below says the same pair.
  window's near end (IDLE_CACHE_ENABLE=0): the SCTLR.C write is NOT in this image - the wrapper
      calls entry_window_note(win, entry_sctlr()) with the register unchanged, so _cwe_win and
      _cwe_set are the SAME value by construction and the pair says only that the site ran.
      **Agreement there is this arm's expected reading, not a failed enable.** The 526 narration
      below predates this line; where its checklist and this derived line differ, this one governs.
  window's far end (EXIT_POC_FLUSH=0): no exit-side flush of this image's own. That is an absence of
      THIS flag and not evidence that the inner seam is absent: an arm that flushes inside the real
      exit would arrive as its own switch, and the converse clause above refuses a record carrying a
      switch this gate does not print - so such an arm cannot be booted with nobody having read it.
  the seam (SEAM_POC=0, SEAM_MEASURE=0): NO interception of this image's own - no
      `--wrap=FlushPoU_Dcache`, so no wrapper of ours sits between the real exit's `push {fp, lr}`
      and its `pop {fp, pc}`, and the flush inside that function is Apple's own. **Both seam keys are
      at 0 here, which is the only state this paragraph is printed in**: with either at 1 the
      interception is in the link, so a 0 beside a 1 above is an arm and not this. The exit's call is
      untested by such a run: this is the arm 568 already ran (533's configuration), so a record with
      both keys at 0 is a re-run of that arm and not a test of 535 - 547 section 5's seam is unprobed,
      and an absent flag here is an absent interception, not an absent problem.
  interrupt stack (ISTACK_SEPARATE=1): separate from the boot thread's, so an interrupt-path
      fault is not a fault on the stack the boot is using.
  idle stack (IDLE_STACK=1): the wrapper is in this image.
  and the count keys are a schedule, never a total: n publishes while n <= 4 and then at the powers
      of two (entry_slot_publish, entry_stubs.c:6236), so a published 4 means AT LEAST four - which
      is why a count read as a total is 406's tell and not a reading.

